in a completely different
mood, like so inspired,

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.560
so motivated or comforted or whatnot.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.940
And so I'm looking forward to it as well

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:42.600
just to be like completely present

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:45.843
and go wherever our conversation may.

00:10:45.843 --> 00:10:46.676
- Yup. Yup.

00:10:46.676 --> 00:10:47.973
I'm 100% with you on that.

00:10:47.973 --> 00:10:50.490
And I think it really speaks
to that level of community too.

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:53.640
I mean, obviously community
looks very different these days

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:54.630
and it has in the past

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:57.270
and there's all these
conversations and arguments about

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:58.810
can you still have that community by Zoom?

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:01.250
Do you have to be in person
and there's valid pieces

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:03.330
to all that but I think
there's still space

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:05.510
for kind of deep authenticity

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:07.272
that doesn't have to
always happen in person.

00:11:07.272 --> 00:11:08.105
I mean, people have been doing that

00:11:08.105 --> 00:11:09.440
through phone calls, through texts.

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:10.830
And so it really, I think

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:12.880
in some ways it's given us
opportunities to really dig

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.540
into that in another way for me personally

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:17.180
not to get too general.

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:18.517
And so I've seen that
even throughout COVID

00:11:18.517 --> 00:11:21.480
I have family that's spread
out all over the world.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.410
So the gatherings we've been
able to have at holidays

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:26.270
we wouldn't have had another time,

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:28.260
just being able to connect
with people in ways

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:30.240
and family members that we
wouldn't have had before

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:31.380
grant is still takes a level

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:32.530
of us being willing to go

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:34.530
to that place of authenticity when we get

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:36.000
on these Zoom calls and things like that.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:39.110
But I think there's a
tremendous opportunity there.

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:42.370
- Yeah. The weird thing though about Zoom

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:46.140
or FaceTime or whatnot
is you feel so connected

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:49.440
and then the minute it's
end or computer shuts

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:52.310
you're alone again in your
house or your apartment and that

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:53.913
I think is really weird.

00:11:55.170 --> 00:12:00.170
And also there's an
element of I don't know

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:05.170
just sensing someone's
aura or their energy.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:08.240
And I miss that, you know

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:11.040
it's possible to have
conversations and go deep

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:15.400
without that but I don't
know, awkward silences.

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:16.820
I weirdly miss those,

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:21.450
I miss feeling tension or whatnot.

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:25.733
And that's stuff I'm excited
for as the world opens back up.

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:28.980
But I think it has
certainly taught us a lot

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:30.201
about connection.

00:12:30.201 --> 00:12:31.034
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:12:31.034 --> 00:12:31.972
And I think that's a great perspective.

00:12:31.972 --> 00:12:34.110
I mean that you're
highlighting kind of both sides

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:35.830
of the coin too 'cause
I definitely even since

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:38.810
as I've had get-togethers
outside with students, you know

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:40.310
over the semester it's
just a different level

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:41.660
of energy that comes through.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:43.290
And it's almost like that just

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:45.650
that one hour meeting kind
of changes like you said

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:46.910
it's trajectory of everyone's day.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:49.360
There was just a different
pep in people's step.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:50.193
I know I'm sounding

00:12:50.193 --> 00:12:53.240
like I'm 50 years old right
now, but that's okay. (laughs)

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:55.010
But you know, just what that did

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:56.727
to really change people's
attitude during the day.

00:12:56.727 --> 00:12:59.010
Just that in-person connection.

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.580
It's funny you mentioned the awkwardness,

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:01.520
the silent awkwardness

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:03.420
'cause those pieces are
definitely there too

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:05.910
but even in the way those
were energizing too.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.990
So there's a lot of learning, yeah

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:09.202
- Definitely.

00:13:09.202 --> 00:13:13.043
I feel like learning so much
and there's weirdly like

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:18.880
obviously I don't wish that the past year

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:22.010
like I don't wish that
had happened to our world

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:25.260
but if I'm trying to find a
silver lining and like accept

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.180
that this did happen
and this is what it is

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:31.570
there are so many things
that I think I've learned

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.300
and ways I've grown.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:36.670
And I don't know if I would
have had those realizations

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:40.010
or that time or those family connections

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:42.520
or family issues addressed

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:45.900
had there not been such silence,

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:50.900
panic and claustrophobia
at one time, you know?

00:13:51.251 --> 00:13:54.779
So I'm definitely
leaving like the pandemic

00:13:54.779 --> 00:13:55.930
I literally got a coffee today

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:57.070
without a mask for the first time.

00:13:57.070 --> 00:13:57.903
It was so weird.

00:13:57.903 --> 00:14:00.453
I felt like that was big for me.

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:02.383
I definitely feel

00:14:02.383 --> 00:14:04.893
like there's so much I
learned throughout this time.

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:08.380
- Well, that's really
encouraging for me to hear too.

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:11.390
And I think even for our
audience, because in a lot of ways

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:13.610
just everything that you've
done over the last few years

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:15.160
you've been at such a high level

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.950
of sharing your stories
so openly and honestly,

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:19.430
I think people are getting a lot of that

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:21.870
from you both to even hear
that you're still learning

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:25.570
in the process I think
is just really beneficial

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:26.990
for people to be able to hear as well

00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:30.120
to know that even someone like
you who's had such a journey

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.020
and has learned so much
there's always more to learn.

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:34.110
It's not like this is
kind a one-time thing

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:36.309
with that just ended in a sense.

00:14:36.309 --> 00:14:37.480
And that's the end of the story.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:41.030
- Definitely. And that's
something that I have

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:42.710
to remind myself of.

00:14:42.710 --> 00:14:44.830
Like I had an instance

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.110
I gave a keynote virtually

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:49.580
to the University of Wisconsin

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.660
on mental health and
body image these issues.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:54.510
And that's a big school for me.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:57.700
Like that's a power five
conference school, you know

00:14:57.700 --> 00:14:59.490
I am it's Wisconsin.

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:02.720
And then I'm so the whole few days before

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:03.560
and the day of like

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:06.950
I just had such imposter
syndrome, I was like I mean

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:08.360
and a whole other conversation

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:10.030
is giving a keynote virtually.

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:12.300
I mean, it's already a lose,

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:14.320
like no one's showing up to Zoom

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:16.690
prepared to be fully
engaged for 45 minutes.

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:18.880
Whereas they be when they
walk into an auditorium

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:20.810
like okay, I'll invest my emotions.

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:21.910
I'll open my heart.

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:23.070
It's so that's weird.

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:25.660
And then I'm not just like
presenting a math presentation.

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:27.320
I'm like opening up my heart

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:29.140
which is even harder
because I'm like, hello

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:31.270
is anyone on the other side of the screen?

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:32.230
You see people fidgeting,

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:34.360
turning off their cameras
is really difficult.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:35.810
So that's an element.

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:38.480
And then I'm I have that
imposter syndrome of like

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:39.860
what if they don't like
what I have to say?

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:41.400
What if I don't touch someone?

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:42.233
What if I don't move them?

00:15:42.233 --> 00:15:44.830
What if I lose sight

00:15:44.830 --> 00:15:47.880
and then I had like a last
minute therapy session that day.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:51.730
Like I happen to be able to get
squeezed in by my therapist.

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:56.620
And she said to me,

00:15:56.620 --> 00:16:01.270
but isn't that what you
speak about this right now?

00:16:01.270 --> 00:16:02.650
Like why do you feel

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:05.050
like you have to pretend
this isn't happening?

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:08.090
You know, why don't you
like open up your keynote

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:10.430
in your intro with like guess where I was

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:13.060
at one o'clock today, like therapy.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:16.290
And so I, after they read my introduction

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:19.550
I was like, I gotta admit today

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:22.140
it feels really weird to hear
my intro because I cringe

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:23.940
at like the accomplishments
and the whatnot

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:25.360
because I still don't feel

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:27.610
like good enough that I
even did things, you know?

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:31.890
And then it just really
made me feel lighter

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:33.530
continuing with the presentation

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:36.120
because my big fear
that I'm not good enough

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:36.980
and I'm an imposter.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:40.200
Well, guess what I told everyone
in the first two minutes

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:42.200
and I also think I did gain some respect

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:45.520
'cause they were like, whoa,
this is TMI from this lady.

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:47.080
What else does she have to say?

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:50.180
So I think going back
to how we even got here

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:55.180
in the first place is I feel very okay

00:16:56.620 --> 00:16:59.140
in not having the answers and
I'm always trying to learn

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:01.590
but I definitely have to
remind the people who follow me

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:04.170
about all the time
because they do come to me

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:07.300
as if I have all the answers
or that I'm totally healthy

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.834
and done with depression
and body image issues

00:17:09.834 --> 00:17:13.930
or there's an expectation
of like I'm always happy.

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:16.996
I'm always on top of my
anxiety and my sadness and my

00:17:16.996 --> 00:17:18.990
and it's just not the case.

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:21.970
And so I feel like I'm
always saying back, hi

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:26.950
I know some stuff, but I'm
still as lost as you. (laughs)

00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:28.630
- Yeah, I think that goes a long way.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:30.450
I imagine that folks can
really resonate with that

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:33.290
especially when they're walking
through it at the same time.

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:35.890
And it's just it's just good to hear that.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.710
Not good in the sense that
like oh, this is great

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:38.543
but good in the sense

00:17:38.543 --> 00:17:41.640
that it's authentic and it
empowers other people too

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:43.100
just everything you're going through.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:43.933
So I'm also, you know

00:17:43.933 --> 00:17:45.420
there may be a few members of our audience

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:46.690
who don't know your story too.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:48.520
So I'd be curious if you
could just share with them

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:52.270
especially along this journey
and the way that you just

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:53.700
even talked about the imposter syndrome.

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:54.533
That's definitely real

00:17:54.533 --> 00:17:58.130
and so big at times I've
had similar situations

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:01.290
where I've shared my story
as a black neuroscientist

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:03.770
with imposter syndrome in a Zoom setting

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:06.480
to hundreds of virtual people I can't see.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:09.470
So definitely relate
to you on that aspect.

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:11.900
But like with all of
that, how did you even get

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:15.190
to this place of wanting
to share your story?

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:16.830
Especially because it was already hard

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:17.920
and with all those challenges

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:21.140
or how did you move into this
space of saying, okay, well

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:22.370
I'm gonna take this imposter syndrome.

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:23.510
I'm still gonna talk about it.

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:26.571
What gave you that strength?

00:18:26.571 --> 00:18:28.310
- So just to stop myself

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:30.870
from speaking for the next hour nonstop

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:34.263
I'll give you like the
elevator pitch of my story.

00:18:35.392 --> 00:18:40.392
I essentially went to USC to
play volleyball and on paper

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:42.990
you know that was everything
I had worked wanted

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:46.750
out of high school, you
know, Pac-12, great team,

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:49.480
great school academics like had friends

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:51.200
like this is what I was supposed to do.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:53.770
Like I'm a successful teenager,

00:18:53.770 --> 00:18:56.620
but then I started to realize

00:18:56.620 --> 00:19:00.100
that no matter how many
boxes you check off in life

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:02.010
that's not the answer to happiness,

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:03.930
fulfillment, meaning, purpose

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:06.330
all those important things
that we really need.

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:08.530
And it took me a while to learn that.

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:12.910
But my freshman year I
felt so much pressure.

00:19:12.910 --> 00:19:15.460
It's like I finally made it to cloud nine

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.910
and then it was okay well,
let's survive at cloud nine.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.330
And we were number one
team in the country.

00:19:20.330 --> 00:19:22.750
National Player of The Year
is on the court with me,

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:25.080
we're in the first 22 games,

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:28.270
I'm adjusting to the
college academic level.

00:19:28.270 --> 00:19:29.960
I'm always missing class.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:31.550
I'm on planes every other week.

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:34.520
I mean, it was just a whole new lifestyle

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:38.020
and so much demand that
I was not prepared for.

00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:40.020
And that I did not know how to handle.

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:43.420
And so as a result, I
developed performance anxiety

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:49.780
intertwined with some binge
eating as coping, and then going

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:53.600
through that for two
years, I didn't, oh, sorry.

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:56.560
Going for that for those
two things for one year

00:19:56.560 --> 00:20:00.610
I didn't know what was happening to me.

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:02.730
You know, I was like why
am I crying before games?

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:04.100
Why am I so nervous to play?

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:05.570
Why is my heart racing?

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:08.100
I didn't even know performance
anxiety was a thing.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:09.720
I thought people had social anxiety.

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:11.240
I really didn't understand the scope.

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:12.430
And I was like, I talk to people.

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:13.263
I make friends.

00:20:13.263 --> 00:20:14.096
Like I don't have that.

00:20:14.096 --> 00:20:15.880
So I was like, what's happening to me.

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:17.307
There was also that look at me

00:20:17.307 --> 00:20:18.880
and I'm going off on my hour rant.

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:20.440
I'm almost done.

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:24.360
I'm like there's also this pressure

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:28.690
and this idea of what an athlete is

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:31.690
a D one athlete at USC, Pac-12 champions.

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:33.530
You know, you don't have
weakness, you get the job done.

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:35.330
You don't make excuses, you figure it out.

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:39.560
So I also felt really
insecure and not valid

00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:42.350
in the anxiety I was facing
and the body image issues.

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:44.220
And then come my sophomore year

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.230
I mean, fell, hit that wall.

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:48.220
And that was depression for me.

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:50.560
And so having gone

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:53.970
so that was kind of how
those things manifested.

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:55.610
But then it was my sophomore year

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:58.360
when I finally was seeing a therapist

00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:01.810
I'd really hit that place of
like, it's either continue

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:05.150
into the darkness which at
this point I'm terrified about

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:09.320
or have that conversation
and wave my white flag

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:12.560
and surrender what I thought
I was doing at the time.

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:15.650
And then in getting help
and starting to get better

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:18.830
and getting on meds and
start to understand, oh

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:20.230
this is what's going on.

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:21.500
This is why it happened.

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:22.900
This is how you can make it better.

00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:25.978
Like you're normal, there's solutions.

00:21:25.978 --> 00:21:27.760
There's ways to go about this.

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:31.120
That's when that next
element of like, whoa

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:34.600
well then why was I so caught off guard?

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:36.360
Why wasn't I aware of this?

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:38.840
Why didn't I know it was okay.

00:21:38.840 --> 00:21:41.950
And then that's really
what sparked my desire

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:46.950
to share was truly so
that no other athlete

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:50.450
had to go through what I did.

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:53.350
You know, that we could say
something before it happened

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:58.230
and you know that confidence
didn't come out of nowhere.

00:21:58.230 --> 00:22:00.750
I was journalism major.

00:22:00.750 --> 00:22:01.710
I love storytelling.

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:03.460
I love being in front of cameras.

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:06.110
So once I understood and came to terms

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:08.870
with what had happened
to me and accepted it,

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:12.400
then the normal personality
that I had kicked in of like,

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:15.130
well let's talk about this,
let's bring attention to it.

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:16.690
So then I delivered that TED talk

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:18.300
which is a whole another story

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:20.850
but it wasn't as easy
as I just made it sound.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:22.130
And then that from that point on

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:24.290
was really finding my voice.

00:22:24.290 --> 00:22:25.710
- Yeah. That's great in so many ways.

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:28.333
I mean, it sounds like there
was an awakening in a sense

00:22:28.333 --> 00:22:32.400
I mean it sounds like part of
that was the healthy fear too

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:33.487
of where things could go

00:22:33.487 --> 00:22:34.900
and that you've just decided

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:37.296
you didn't want to be in that place.

00:22:37.296 --> 00:22:40.800
- Yeah. You know, I think the thing

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:45.260
that I can always come back to is

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:47.970
my young life like as a teenager

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:51.500
I never understood why
people would kill themselves.

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:52.590
I didn't get it.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:54.580
It was like but life is so great.

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:56.220
Why wouldn't she wanna live?

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:57.810
Why wouldn't you wanna be alive.

00:22:57.810 --> 00:23:00.720
And then it was my sophomore year

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:05.660
I remember thinking to
myself, oh now I know.

00:23:05.660 --> 00:23:08.680
Now I know why people don't wanna be here

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:10.100
and they don't wanna live.

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:13.760
And that was my whoa.

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:15.790
We are somewhere else now.

00:23:15.790 --> 00:23:20.470
And you know, that was for me

00:23:20.470 --> 00:23:24.180
that like awareness of
okay, I've tried to deal

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:26.573
with this myself and
it's only gotten worse.

00:23:27.861 --> 00:23:31.190
And of course now I'm at
a place where like, yes

00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:33.630
I have sympathy and so much compassion

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:38.500
and love for those who
are unable to keep living

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:41.210
but I don't feel that way anymore

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:42.520
which is why I'm so passionate

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:44.340
about like pulling
someone out of those steps

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:47.177
because I've been in the
depths and I'm out of them.

00:23:47.177 --> 00:23:50.460
And so I want people to know
you can escape those feelings.

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:51.360
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:54.290
So important that realization
I think that goes a long way.

00:23:54.290 --> 00:23:56.180
You know, obviously as we
talk about a lot of times

00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:59.250
the fact that people are just
trying to escape the pain

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:01.500
because they can't figure
out how to deal with it.

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:03.210
So I think the fact that
you have that awareness

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:04.820
and can kind of meet people there.

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:06.610
And then also that the
fact that you're talking

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:08.600
about all the things that
you wish you'd been aware

00:24:08.600 --> 00:24:11.680
of beforehand, because you just
thought things were normal.

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:14.710
You didn't know necessarily
what was going on at a time.

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:15.543
And so the fact

00:24:15.543 --> 00:24:16.440
that you're kind of empowering others

00:24:16.440 --> 00:24:20.090
to tackle things head-on early
on I think is so important.

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:20.923
I'm curious even if you know

00:24:20.923 --> 00:24:22.440
if you're willing to elaborate more just

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:23.870
in terms of 'cause you
know student athletes

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:25.810
who may be listening in like what is

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:27.690
how do you find that line

00:24:27.690 --> 00:24:32.290
between having a high level
of performance and expectation

00:24:32.290 --> 00:24:34.400
but then knowing when to pull back

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:36.440
because as I've heard you talk
about lots of times, you know

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:38.610
as an athlete there is that fine line of

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:41.110
there is someone else
that's ready to replace you

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:42.050
if you kind of slip

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:45.250
in your performance or
if there's that window.

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:46.430
So I mean, that's to me

00:24:46.430 --> 00:24:49.810
that doesn't sound like
an easy decision to make

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:53.220
and I'd expect that extends
beyond sports as well.

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:55.930
I mean, people who are in
tense work environments,

00:24:55.930 --> 00:24:57.770
tense academic settings

00:24:57.770 --> 00:24:59.580
even to take it a scope a
little bit for them thinking

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:00.610
about some of the things
we've talked about

00:25:00.610 --> 00:25:03.410
at this podcast in terms
of minorities who are

00:25:03.410 --> 00:25:05.210
in situations where they're the only one

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:08.600
there's this huge weight
to kind of represent kind

00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:10.260
of a bigger culture than yourself

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:11.960
and that all those tensions
I'm just curious, you know

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:12.793
in your own experience

00:25:12.793 --> 00:25:16.859
how do you suggest that
people kind of figure out

00:25:16.859 --> 00:25:20.904
that line about when to seek help?

00:25:20.904 --> 00:25:21.737
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:25:21.737 --> 00:25:24.720
I have a few things I'll share on that.

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:27.260
I will preface this by saying I'll speak

00:25:27.260 --> 00:25:31.190
from the perspective of a
competitive student athlete

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:34.660
because that's my experience
and I never wanna act

00:25:34.660 --> 00:25:37.770
like I understood even the surface

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:39.810
of the struggles, you know minority

00:25:39.810 --> 00:25:42.630
or someone without white
privilege might be experiencing.

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:44.560
So recognize that.

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:46.100
And don't wanna even act

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:47.990
like I could elaborate on
answering that question,

00:25:47.990 --> 00:25:49.180
but to the experiences

00:25:49.180 --> 00:25:51.160
of like a high-performing student athlete

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:53.390
or high-performing academic student

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:56.400
what I would say is, you know

00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:01.400
the way that sports culture
teaches you to work hard

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:04.900
and like the way they
communicate work ethic

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:05.980
is it's the grind.

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:09.790
It's you always go one more,
you always push harder.

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:12.000
You do the best don't rest.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:13.540
You know, unless this is one

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:16.290
of the most absurd quotes
that used to motivate me,

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:19.660
unless you puke, faint or die, keep going.

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:22.670
I mean, that is something
that I had on my locker

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:24.370
like that is something athletes use.

00:26:24.370 --> 00:26:28.150
And it's also praised
by like the G.O.A.T.S

00:26:28.150 --> 00:26:30.830
which I can also get into later

00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:33.460
because Kobe Bryan actually has expressed

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:34.500
different thoughts on this.

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:35.550
You know, things get misconstrued.

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:40.150
But first thing I'd say is
the way that it's communicated

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:43.820
to us is go, go, go, go, go, never stop.

00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:46.450
And any excuses or pull back is weakness.

00:26:46.450 --> 00:26:48.330
So that's the first problem is

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:50.210
like we're being taught the wrong thing.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:51.360
It's an impossible standard.

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:52.193
It doesn't work.

00:26:52.193 --> 00:26:54.560
The second piece that
I'd actually be curious

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:56.250
your thoughts on this.

00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:57.110
And I learned this

00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:59.850
from one of my favorite
sports psychologists,

00:26:59.850 --> 00:27:03.340
Dr. Michael Gervais,
but there's a difference

00:27:03.340 --> 00:27:06.210
between mental health
and mental toughness.

00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:08.900
And the line often gets very blurry.

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:11.110
And the difference is not communicated

00:27:11.110 --> 00:27:12.850
to athletes nor to coaches.

00:27:12.850 --> 00:27:14.480
Coaches most of the time are the ones

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:15.460
who asked this question

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:17.480
because they don't understand like

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:19.070
how do I know when my player is depressed

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:22.760
or when he's just not trying,
or she's just not doing this.

00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:25.760
So mental health is obviously

00:27:25.760 --> 00:27:28.870
like your general state of
mind, your general wellbeing.

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:30.780
Like let's imagine that
as the base, if you think

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:33.300
of physical health ironically
it's easy to understand

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:35.530
you know, someone physically healthy.

00:27:35.530 --> 00:27:38.560
Then mental toughness is
something that can be trained.

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:40.410
It's a skill, it's with reps,

00:27:40.410 --> 00:27:41.820
with time with putting yourself

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.880
in the trenches with overcoming adversity

00:27:43.880 --> 00:27:46.173
time and time again you
get tougher mentally.

00:27:47.060 --> 00:27:48.770
However, the way they relate

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:51.960
to like peak performance
and being an elite athlete

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:54.420
or an elite performer in any capacity

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:57.670
is mental health is the foundation.

00:27:57.670 --> 00:28:02.070
And then mental toughness is
the bridge to peak performance,

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:03.970
sustainable peak performance.

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:08.970
So athletes have to
understand is am I working

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.990
at my base or am I building
my bridge right now?

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:17.990
And that's something that I
think each person has to learn

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:19.560
for themselves.

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:22.543
And they have to be
willing to identify that.

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:28.440
To give an example my freshman
year I started and I played

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:30.660
but I wasn't in the position I wanted.

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:32.940
I was like in a second string role

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:34.740
but not the first string role.

00:28:34.740 --> 00:28:37.600
And I remember similar to that mentality

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:39.650
I was like okay, when she's
in the gym I'm in the gym.

00:28:39.650 --> 00:28:41.150
If she's passing, I'm passing.

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:44.283
If she goes, I go, like,
I will outwork you.

00:28:45.270 --> 00:28:48.390
And as a result, all of my
choices and actions were based

00:28:48.390 --> 00:28:50.890
on outworking her not actually focusing on

00:28:50.890 --> 00:28:52.720
what was best for me.

00:28:52.720 --> 00:28:55.500
And so I got anxious, I overworked myself.

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:58.880
I wasn't listening to my body,
listening to my training,

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:01.370
then come my senior year

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:02.880
I'm in that first string position.

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:06.100
I've done all this work and
what do you know that girl

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:08.260
behind me she's staying extra.

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:10.750
She's doing this and that.

00:29:10.750 --> 00:29:13.050
And I don't know if it
was motivated by me.

00:29:13.050 --> 00:29:14.000
She could have just been doing it.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:15.940
But I felt that in me, oh my gosh,

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:18.540
you know my backup is doing these things.

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:19.373
Should I stay?

00:29:19.373 --> 00:29:21.410
And I was like, you know what, no

00:29:21.410 --> 00:29:24.330
I gave my all for three hours.

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:26.150
I feel like I had a good practice.

00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:27.800
And I think it's more beneficial

00:29:27.800 --> 00:29:31.360
for me to call it a day,
grab my water shower

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:33.780
and go eat dinner with my
roommate because I put my work in

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:35.030
I feel good about that.

00:29:35.030 --> 00:29:36.960
You know, it has to be based on you.

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:39.730
And another thing that comes
to mind and you mentioned this

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:44.020
in the question was this idea
of like holding back and one

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:47.907
of my favorite quotes from
Kobe Bryant's recent novel

00:29:47.907 --> 00:29:49.550
"Geese Are Never Swans"

00:29:49.550 --> 00:29:54.070
is it takes more strength to hold back

00:29:54.070 --> 00:29:55.870
than it does to push.

00:29:55.870 --> 00:29:58.280
The easy thing is to go one.

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:00.400
Yeah. The easy thing is to go one more

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:01.470
to do what coach says.

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:03.550
The easy thing is to buy in.

00:30:03.550 --> 00:30:06.730
The harder thing is to
speak up for yourself.

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:09.610
The harder thing is to say,
I don't have this in me.

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:13.000
I'm working at Efuel and I've
been there for two days now.

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:14.100
I need to rest.

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:15.470
I need recovery.

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:19.960
So lots of points there in an answer

00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:24.030
to your question, I would
invite everyone to explore.

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:26.450
Are you working with your mental health?

00:30:26.450 --> 00:30:28.800
Is this an issue of mental toughness?

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:30.900
And can you play with that

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:33.590
and use your experiences to help guide you

00:30:33.590 --> 00:30:36.073
to when you're making the right call?

00:30:36.073 --> 00:30:37.110
- Yeah, I think that's so helpful.

00:30:37.110 --> 00:30:38.320
I mean, you cover a lot of ground there.

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:39.440
I think it's all all great.

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:40.390
Especially that distinction

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:43.030
